      "heading": "4. Understanding the CSS Examination Structure",
      "body": "The CSS competitive examination is a rigorous multi-stage selection process designed to evaluate a candidate’s cognitive breadth, written communication, analytical clarity, psychological temperament, and physical fitness. The process is divided into five distinct stages: 1) Stage 1: MCQ-Based Preliminary Test (MPT) — Introduced in 2022 as a mandatory pre-selection filter, the MPT consists of 200 multiple-choice questions (200 marks) administered over 200 minutes with no negative marking. The syllabus covers English, General Abilities/Basic Mathematics, General Knowledge (Everyday Science, Current Affairs, Pakistan Affairs), Islamic Studies/Civics, and Urdu. Candidates must score at least 33% (66 marks out of 200) to qualify. The MPT is purely qualifying; its marks do not contribute to the written or final merit ranking. 2) Stage 2: Written Examination — Qualified candidates sit for a comprehensive 1,200-mark written examination comprising six compulsory subjects (600 marks) and optional subjects totaling 600 marks. Each paper lasts three hours and tests conceptual depth, critical argumentation, and structured presentation. 3) Stage 3: Medical Examination — Candidates who qualify in the written examination undergo a detailed physical and medical examination by the Central Medical Board (CMB) to certify that they are physically fit and free from any health condition likely to interfere with the efficient performance of public duties. 4) Stage 4: Psychological Assessment — This phase spans two full days and involves written psychological instruments, personality questionnaires, aptitude tests, group discussions, and personal interviews conducted by occupational psychologists. The assessment produces an objective psychological profile that assists the interview board. 5) Stage 5: Viva Voce (Interview) — Candidates appear before an FPSC Interview Board headed by the Commission Chairman or Senior Members for a 300-mark oral examination. The Viva Voce evaluates general intelligence, moral traits, alertness, balance of mind, leadership qualities, and knowledge of national and international affairs. The minimum qualifying benchmark in Viva Voce is 100 marks (33.3%). The final national and provincial merit list is determined by the aggregate score out of 1,500 marks (1,200 Written + 300 Viva Voce)."
